(*
 * t-rust-db -- unified SQL grammar source of truth
 *
 * Folded in from the standalone `t-rust-db/grammar` repo (db-core#43) --
 * see this crate's `ALIGNMENT.md` for what was checked against sqlite-rs
 * along the way, and this repo's git history / the archived grammar
 * repo for how this file's shape (one file, three sections) was arrived
 * at.
 *
 * ONE file, THREE sections, replacing the earlier column-rs.ebnf +
 * sqlite-rs.ebnf split:
 *
 *   1. SHARED         -- productions genuinely, word-for-word identical
 *                        AND self-contained (no reference into a rule
 *                        that itself differs per engine) between the two
 *                        grammars. Defined once, referenced by name from
 *                        both sections below.
 *   2. COLUMN-RS ONLY -- db-core/sql-parser's `column` Cargo-feature
 *                        section: column-rs's analytics-over-Parquet
 *                        subset (SELECT only, no writes).
 *   3. SQLITE-RS ONLY (including DDL) -- db-core/sql-parser's `row`
 *                        Cargo-feature section: sqlite-rs's own grammar,
 *                        migrated in unchanged (`db-core#23`) -- DDL,
 *                        DML, transactions, PRAGMA, and sqlite-rs's much
 *                        richer expression grammar.
 *
 * Sections 2 and 3 each remain a complete, self-contained grammar when
 * read on its own -- exactly the content of the two former files, just
 * placed under one roof. Some rule NAMES repeat across sections (both
 * define `sql-stmt`, `expr`, `or-expr`, `table-name`, `column-ref`, ...)
 * because both grammars model the same SQL concepts under the same
 * conventional names -- scoped by section, the same way the two former
 * files each had their own `expr` without colliding. That is NOT the
 * same thing as the SHARED section below, whose rules are deduplicated
 * because they are provably identical everywhere they're used.
 *
 * What actually qualifies as SHARED, and what doesn't
 * ----------------------------------------------------
 * A rule only moves to SHARED if (a) both engines accept EXACTLY the
 * same input for it, AND (b) it doesn't reference another nonterminal
 * that itself diverges per engine -- otherwise a single "shared"
 * production would silently mean something different depending on which
 * engine's grammar you followed it from. Checked and rejected for that
 * second reason: `expr`/`or-expr`/`and-expr` are textually IDENTICAL in
 * both grammars (`expr ::= or-expr ;`, `or-expr ::= and-expr { "OR"
 * and-expr } ;`, `and-expr ::= not-expr { "AND" not-expr } ;`) -- but
 * each engine's `and-expr` bottoms out into a different `not-expr`
 * (column-rs: `not-expr | comparison`; sqlite-rs: `{ "NOT" }
 * equality-expr`, feeding a much richer operator ladder). A shared
 * `and-expr` would have to pick one `not-expr` to point at, silently
 * misdescribing the other engine. So these stay defined per section
 * below, each self-contained -- genuinely identical text, deliberately
 * NOT deduplicated, for the reason above. Likewise rejected: comparison
 * operators (sqlite-rs also accepts `==`), arithmetic (sqlite-rs's
 * `multiplicative-expr` has `%` and more precedence tiers below it),
 * identifiers (sqlite-rs additionally accepts quoted forms), string
 * literals (sqlite-rs's tokenizer supports `''`-doubling; column-rs's
 * doesn't), and JOIN grammar (sqlite-rs's chain is strictly larger).
 *
 * What IS shared (self-contained, no downstream ambiguity):
 *   - `ident-chars` -- the unquoted-identifier character class. Both
 *     engines' tokenizers accept exactly this for a bare identifier.
 *     sqlite-rs's own `identifier` extends it with quoting (see its
 *     section); column-rs's `IDENT` is exactly this, nothing more.
 *   - `explain-prefix` -- the `EXPLAIN [QUERY PLAN]` token sequence,
 *     pure terminals, identical in both. What it wraps differs per
 *     engine (column-rs: a `select-stmt` only; sqlite-rs: any
 *     `sql-stmt`) -- see each section's own top-level rule.
 *   - `comparison-op` -- the 7 comparison operators both engines accept
 *     identically (`=`, `!=`, `<>`, `<`, `<=`, `>`, `>=`). Pure
 *     terminals, no nonterminal reference, so -- unlike the operator
 *     LEVELS that use it -- it's genuinely safe to share. See its own
 *     entry below for what each engine does with it beyond the shared
 *     set (sqlite-rs adds `==` and splits the set across two precedence
 *     tiers; column-rs treats it as one flat, non-chainable tier).
 *
 * Tried harder and still rejected: can `additive-expr`'s SHAPE (not
 * just its operator set) be shared, given both engines' real parser
 * functions (`additive` in column.rs, `additive_expr` in
 * row/grammar.rs) do the exact same precedence-climbing fold over `+`/
 * `-`? The shape is identical -- but `additive-expr`'s body must name
 * its own next-level operand (`multiplicative`/`multiplicative-expr`),
 * and that's exactly the same "shared rule referencing a per-engine
 * rule" problem `and-expr` hit above, one level down. A shared
 * `additive-expr` would need either (a) a real generic/parameterized
 * nonterminal, which plain EBNF has no notation for, or (b) forcing
 * column-rs's `primary` to be re-described as walking through sqlite-
 * rs's `concat-expr`/`collate-expr`/`unary-expr` wrapper levels it
 * doesn't actually have real parser functions for -- which would break
 * this file's own founding principle (both former files: "structural
 * re-derivation of the actual parser... every rule has a corresponding
 * parser function") for the sake of a shorter grammar file. Kept
 * separate; not a missed opportunity, a checked and rejected one.
 *
 * Maintenance rule: any change to either engine's grammar (`db-core/
 * sql-parser::column` or `::row` / sqlite-rs's own parser) MUST update
 * the corresponding section of THIS file in the same PR/commit. Before
 * touching a SHARED rule, re-verify it's still identical AND still
 * self-contained in both engines -- if a change breaks either
 * condition, move it back into each engine's own section rather than
 * leaving a "shared" rule that's only true for one of them.
 *
 * Notation: ::=  rule definition          |    alternation
 *           []   optional (0 or 1)        {}   repetition (0 or more)
 *           ""   literal keyword/punct (keywords case-insensitive)
 *           UPPERCASE  lexer token
 *)

(* ================================================================
 * ===== 1. SHARED =====
 * ================================================================
 *)

ident-chars     ::= ( ALPHA | "_" ) { ALPHA | DIGIT | "_" } ;
                     (* the unquoted-identifier character shape, accepted
                        identically by both tokenizers. *)

explain-prefix  ::= "EXPLAIN" [ "QUERY" "PLAN" ] ;
                     (* identical token sequence in both grammars. What
                        it wraps differs per engine -- see each section's
                        own top-level statement rule. *)

comparison-op   ::= "=" | "!=" | "<>" | "<" | "<=" | ">" | ">=" ;
                     (* the 7 operators both engines accept identically.
                        column-rs's own `comp-op` (section 2) is exactly
                        this set, used at one flat, non-chainable
                        precedence tier. sqlite-rs (section 3) splits
                        this SAME set across two tiers -- `equality-tail`
                        gets `=`/`<>`/`!=` (plus its own `==` synonym,
                        NOT part of this shared set), `relational-expr`
                        gets `<`/`<=`/`>`/`>=` -- and allows chaining
                        (`a < b < c`) where column-rs's parser rejects a
                        second comparison outright. Same operator
                        vocabulary, different precedence policy and
                        cardinality -- see each section for its own use
                        of this set. *)

(* ================================================================
 * ===== 2. COLUMN-RS ONLY =====
 * db-core/sql-parser's `column` Cargo-feature section
 * (db-core/sql-parser/src/column.rs) -- column-rs's analytics-over-
 * Parquet subset: SELECT only, no DDL/DML, no transactions, no PRAGMA,
 * no CTEs. Every rule here has a corresponding parser function; nothing
 * is listed as supported that parse()/parse_explain() cannot actually
 * accept. Self-contained: every rule referenced here is defined in this
 * section or in SHARED above.
 * ================================================================
 *)

sql-stmt        ::= [ explain-prefix ] select-stmt ;
                     (* the ONLY statement form -- no INSERT/UPDATE/
                        DELETE/DDL/transactions/PRAGMA; column-rs reads
                        Parquet, it never writes. `explain-prefix`: see
                        SHARED above -- unlike sqlite-rs, only ever wraps
                        a `select-stmt`. *)

(* ----- SELECT ----- *)

select-stmt     ::= "SELECT" [ "DISTINCT" ] select-list
                     "FROM" from-clause
                     { join-clause }
                     [ where-clause ]
                     [ group-by-clause ]
                     [ order-by-clause ]
                     [ limit-clause ] ;
                     (* #47: `DISTINCT` dedups the fully-projected output
                        rows -- planned by setting a flag on the terminal
                        `Finalize` step (ADR 0007) rather than a new opcode.
                        Combined with `group-by-clause`, dedup runs *after*
                        the GROUP BY hash-aggregate merge, matching DuckDB's
                        semantics of DISTINCT as a post-aggregate dedup. *)

select-list     ::= select-item { "," select-item } ;

select-item     ::= "*"
                   | column-ref
                   | agg-call
                   | window-call ;
                     (* bare `*` expands to every column of the query's
                        table(s), resolved by the caller/executor (which
                        has the schema) at compile time -- sql-parser has
                        no access to a table's schema itself, so `*` is
                        carried through as an AST-level marker
                        (sql_expr::SelectItem::Star) rather than expanded
                        here. No arbitrary expressions in the select list
                        beyond `*`, a bare column, an aggregate call, or a
                        window call *)

agg-call        ::= agg-func "(" ( column-ref | "*" ) ")" ;
                     (* `*` only valid for COUNT -- COUNT(*); enforced at
                        the AST/execution layer, not the grammar *)

agg-func        ::= "COUNT" | "SUM" | "AVG" | "MIN" | "MAX" ;

window-call     ::= ranking-call | offset-call | value-call | agg-over-call ;

ranking-call    ::= ( "ROW_NUMBER" | "RANK" | "DENSE_RANK" ) "(" ")"
                     "OVER" over-clause ;

offset-call     ::= ( "LAG" | "LEAD" ) "(" column-ref [ "," INTEGER ] ")"
                     "OVER" over-clause ;
                     (* INTEGER is the offset, default 1 when omitted --
                        the default is applied by the AST layer, not here *)

value-call      ::= ( "FIRST_VALUE" | "LAST_VALUE" ) "(" column-ref ")"
                     "OVER" over-clause ;

agg-over-call   ::= ( "SUM" | "AVG" | "COUNT" ) "(" column-ref ")"
                     "OVER" over-clause ;

over-clause     ::= "(" [ "PARTITION" "BY" ident-list ]
                         [ "ORDER" "BY" order-item { "," order-item } ]
                     ")" ;

(* ----- FROM / JOIN -----
 * Equi-joins only (except CROSS, which has no condition), one ON clause
 * per non-CROSS join. Table aliases are a bare trailing identifier (no
 * `AS`) resolved at parse time: `FROM orders o` lets `o.col` stand for
 * `orders.col` anywhere in the query, but the alias itself never appears
 * in the parsed AST (sql_expr::Query/Join only ever hold real table
 * names) -- it's rewritten away before the AST is built. No comma-join,
 * no NATURAL, no USING(...), no subqueries in FROM. Compare sqlite-rs's
 * own `from-clause`/`join-op` in section 3 -- a strictly larger chain
 * this is a narrow subset of; NOT shared (see the file header). *)

from-clause     ::= table-name [ alias ] ;

join-clause     ::= join-kind table-name [ alias ] [ "ON" column-ref "=" column-ref ] ;
                     (* the "ON ..." condition is REQUIRED for every
                        join-kind except "CROSS JOIN", and FORBIDDEN for
                        "CROSS JOIN" (an unconditional cross product) *)

join-kind       ::= "JOIN"
                   | "INNER" "JOIN"
                   | "LEFT" [ "OUTER" ] "JOIN"
                   | "RIGHT" [ "OUTER" ] "JOIN"
                   | "FULL" [ "OUTER" ] "JOIN"
                   | "CROSS" "JOIN" ;
                     (* a query containing a "CROSS JOIN" MUST also have a
                        top-level LIMIT clause -- enforced by the parser,
                        not by this grammar's shape, as a DO-178C
                        bounded-execution rule: an unconditional cross
                        product has no natural row cap the way an
                        equi-join's selectivity gives it *)

alias           ::= IDENT ;
                     (* any IDENT not itself one of this grammar's
                        following keywords (JOIN/INNER/LEFT/RIGHT/FULL/
                        CROSS/OUTER/ON/WHERE/GROUP/ORDER/LIMIT) is taken as
                        an alias -- there is no "AS" keyword *)

(* ----- WHERE -----
 * Two mutually exclusive forms: an expression, or a bare semi-join. The
 * semi-join form must be the ENTIRE where-clause -- it cannot be combined
 * with other conditions via AND/OR (unlike a real SQL engine, where
 * `x IN (...)` is just another boolean-valued expression). *)

where-clause    ::= "WHERE" ( semi-join | expr ) ;

semi-join       ::= column-ref "IN" "(" select-stmt ")" ;
                     (* the subquery is itself a full select-stmt,
                        recursively -- but see select-stmt's caller: a
                        subquery position stops at its closing ")" rather
                        than expecting end-of-input, unlike the top-level
                        statement *)

(* ----- Expressions -----
 * Standard precedence climb: OR < AND < NOT < comparison < concat <
 * additive < multiplicative < unary < primary (db-core#34 added concat/
 * unary). No LIKE/BETWEEN, no CASE.
 *
 * `expr`/`or-expr`/`and-expr` below are textually IDENTICAL to sqlite-
 * rs's own (section 3) -- deliberately NOT moved to SHARED, because
 * `and-expr` bottoms out into `not-expr`, and THIS section's `not-expr`
 * differs from sqlite-rs's. See the file header's "What actually
 * qualifies as SHARED" note. *)

expr            ::= or-expr ;

or-expr         ::= and-expr { "OR" and-expr } ;

and-expr        ::= not-expr { "AND" not-expr } ;

not-expr        ::= "NOT" not-expr
                   | comparison ;
                     (* right-recursive so `NOT NOT x` parses; binds
                        tighter than AND/OR, looser than comparison:
                        `NOT a = b AND c` is `(NOT (a = b)) AND c` *)

comparison      ::= concat [ ( comp-op concat ) | ( "IS" [ "NOT" ] "NULL" ) ] ;
                     (* non-associative: `a = b = c` is not accepted --
                        at most one comparison operator (or one
                        IS [NOT] NULL test) per comparison level *)

comp-op         ::= comparison-op ;
                     (* SHARED with sqlite-rs -- see SHARED above.
                        column-rs collapses this set into one flat,
                        non-chainable precedence tier, where sqlite-rs
                        splits it across two (and adds `==`). *)

concat          ::= additive { "||" additive } ;
                     (* `||` string concatenation, DuckDB/Postgres-style:
                        both operands stringify via `Value`'s `Display`
                        rather than requiring `STRING` (`1 || 'x'` is
                        valid, produces `"1x"`). Binds LOOSER than `+`/
                        `-`/`*`/`/`, matching DuckDB's own precedence --
                        deliberately NOT sqlite-rs's placement (its own
                        `concat-expr` binds tighter than `*`/`/`, a real
                        SQLite quirk column-rs does not follow, since
                        this grammar targets DuckDB parity, not
                        SQLite's). db-core#34. *)

additive        ::= multiplicative { ( "+" | "-" ) multiplicative } ;

multiplicative  ::= unary { ( "*" | "/" ) unary } ;
                     (* no `%` -- see the file header. *)

unary           ::= { "+" | "-" } primary ;
                     (* leading `+`/`-`: `+` is a no-op (not represented
                        in the AST); `-` wraps in a negation node,
                        chainable (`--x` parses). db-core#34. *)

primary         ::= column-ref
                   | INTEGER
                   | FLOAT
                   | STRING
                   | "(" expr ")" ;

(* ----- GROUP BY / ORDER BY / LIMIT ----- *)

group-by-clause ::= "GROUP" "BY" ident-list ;

order-by-clause ::= "ORDER" "BY" column-ref [ "ASC" | "DESC" ] ;
                     (* exactly one column -- no multi-column ORDER BY *)

order-item      ::= column-ref [ "ASC" | "DESC" ] ;
                     (* used inside OVER (...ORDER BY...), which DOES
                        allow multiple columns, unlike the top-level
                        order-by-clause above *)

limit-clause    ::= "LIMIT" INTEGER ;
                     (* non-negative integer only; no OFFSET *)

(* ----- Lexical ----- *)

ident-list      ::= column-ref { "," column-ref } ;

column-ref      ::= IDENT [ "." IDENT ] ;
                     (* `col` or `table.col`; more than one `.` is not a
                        column reference this grammar recognizes (no
                        schema.table.col). sqlite-rs's own `column-ref`
                        (section 3) allows up to 3 parts -- NOT shared. *)

table-name      ::= IDENT ;
                     (* the Parquet file's stem, per column-rs's table
                        resolution -- not validated by the grammar itself *)

IDENT           ::= ident-chars ;
                     (* unquoted only -- no `"quoted"`/`[bracketed]`/
                        `` `backticked` `` forms sqlite-rs's own
                        `identifier` accepts (section 3). `ident-chars`:
                        see SHARED above. *)

INTEGER         ::= DIGIT { DIGIT } ;
FLOAT           ::= DIGIT { DIGIT } "." DIGIT { DIGIT } ;
                     (* exactly one decimal point, digits on both sides
                        -- no `1e5`/`.5`/`1.` forms sqlite-rs's own
                        `NUMBER` accepts (section 3); NOT shared. *)
STRING          ::= "'" { any character except "'" } "'" ;
                     (* no escape sequences, no doubled-quote escaping --
                        NOT shared with sqlite-rs's own `STRING`, whose
                        tokenizer supports `''`-doubling. See the file
                        header. *)

(* ----- Not supported (deliberately) -----
 * Tracked here so the grammar's negative space is visible, not just its
 * positive space. Adding any of these means growing sql-parser's
 * `column` section AND this file in the same change (see Maintenance
 * rule above).
 *
 *   - comma-join, NATURAL JOIN, USING(...)
 *   - subqueries in FROM
 *   - multiple WHERE conditions combined with a semi-join
 *   - LIKE, BETWEEN, CASE
 *   - COLLATE (considered alongside unary minus/concat in db-core#34,
 *     deliberately deferred -- a locale-comparison feature, lower value
 *     for a Parquet analytics engine than the other two; add only if
 *     something concrete needs it)
 *   - multi-column ORDER BY at the top level (only inside OVER (...))
 *   - OFFSET
 *   - INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE/CREATE/DROP/ALTER -- column-rs never writes
 *   - transactions, PRAGMA, CTEs (WITH), views
 *)

(* ================================================================
 * ===== 3. SQLITE-RS ONLY (including DDL) =====
 * db-core/sql-parser's `row` Cargo-feature section
 * (db-core/sql-parser/src/row/*), migrated in unchanged from sqlite-rs's
 * own parser (db-core#23). sqlite-rs itself is being absorbed into the
 * `t-rust-db` org (`t-rust-db/sqlite-rs`) -- see DECISIONS.md.
 * Self-contained: every rule referenced here is defined in this section
 * or in SHARED above.
 *
 * SQLite publishes NO EBNF. The upstream authorities are:
 *   - src/parse.y (Lemon LALR grammar, action-annotated) -- pinned at 3.53.4:
 *     https://github.com/sqlite/sqlite/blob/version-3.53.4/src/parse.y
 *   - Railroad diagrams (human-readable, generated):
 *     https://www.sqlite.org/syntaxdiagrams.html
 *   - Prose reference:
 *     https://www.sqlite.org/lang.html
 * This section is a structural re-derivation of parse.y (no SQLite
 * source text copied) in standard EBNF. Where a rule was distilled from
 * a verified parse.y location it carries `[parse.y:LINE rulename]`;
 * rules added beyond the original spike distillation carry the parse.y
 * rule NAME only.
 *
 * V-BLOCK ANNOTATIONS: every rule is tagged with the value block
 * (plan.md) in which sqlite-rs implements it -- `(* V2 *)`, `(* V3 *)`, ...
 * The grammar thereby tracks the plan's production coverage (V2 ~20%,
 * V3 ~45%, V4 ~80%, V7+ 100%). Rules for future blocks are listed as
 * stubs under "Future blocks" so the denominator is visible.
 * ================================================================
 *)

sql-stmt        ::= select-stmt        (* V2/V6 -- select-stmt now carries an
                                              optional leading with-clause, see below *)
                  | insert-stmt        (* V3 *)
                  | update-stmt        (* V3 *)
                  | delete-stmt        (* V3 *)
                  | create-table-stmt  (* V3 *)
                  | create-index-stmt  (* V3 *)
                  | drop-stmt          (* V3 *)
                  | create-view-stmt   (* V6 *)
                  | drop-view-stmt     (* V6 *)
                  | pragma-stmt        (* V6 *)
                  | analyze-stmt       (* V7 *)
                  | explain-stmt       (* V4 *)
                  | begin-stmt         (* V5 *)
                  | commit-stmt        (* V5 *)
                  | rollback-stmt ;    (* V5 *)
                  (* V4+: CTEs, views -- see Future blocks;
                     #237 landed the INNER/LEFT/CROSS JOIN subset, #250 extended it
                     with NATURAL/RIGHT/FULL/USING/comma-join (see from-clause),
                     #240 landed the UNION ALL compound-select tail (see select-stmt),
                     #243 pulled EXPLAIN [QUERY PLAN] forward from its original
                     V7 slot -- see explain-stmt below -- because the planner's
                     index-selection work needs EQP output to be observable now;
                     general PRAGMA remains V7 (only the narrow
                     journal_mode=WAL|DELETE carve-out landed -- see pragma-stmt
                     below, #388); ANALYZE's bare-name/no-argument forms landed
                     at V7 (#461) -- see analyze-stmt below *)

explain-stmt    ::= explain-prefix sql-stmt ;
                     (* V4 [parse.y:173 explain]. `explain-prefix`: see
                        SHARED above -- unlike column-rs, wraps ANY
                        `sql-stmt`, not just a SELECT. *)

(* ----- SELECT (V2 slice, FROM/JOIN + GROUP BY/HAVING + UNION/UNION ALL V4/V6 slice) -----
 * [parse.y:520 cmd, :654 oneselect, :710 selcollist, :750 from, :925 orderby_opt, :967 limit_opt, :1002 where_opt]
 * V2 restrictions vs full parse.y: no window clause (V9). The FROM
 * clause itself (`from-clause` below) is now the V4 full JOIN slice
 * (#237's INNER/LEFT/CROSS `JOIN ... ON`, extended by #250 with
 * NATURAL, RIGHT/FULL, `USING (...)`, and comma-style `FROM a, b`)
 * rather than V2's single-table-only restriction — subqueries in FROM
 * (#238) remain a future-block stub, see below. `group-by-clause`
 * below is the V4 GROUP BY/HAVING slice (#239). The compound-select
 * tail is the V4 `UNION ALL` slice (#240) plus the V6 plain `UNION`
 * slice (#377/#378) — see compound-op below. `INTERSECT`/`EXCEPT`
 * remain a V7 future-block stub.
 *)

select-stmt     ::= [ with-clause ]                                      (* V6 [parse.y:614 cmd ::= select KW_EXPLAIN? no --
                                                                              select ::= with selectnowith orderby_opt] *)
                     select-core { compound-op select-core }              (* V2 core; V4/V6 [parse.y:623 selectnowith,
                                                                              :645 multiselect_op] compound tail --
                                                                              see compound-op below *)
                     [ "ORDER" "BY" ordering-term { "," ordering-term } ]
                     [ "LIMIT" expr [ ( "OFFSET" | "," ) expr ] ] ;   (* V2/V4 -- ORDER BY/LIMIT bind to the whole
                                                                          compound statement, not any one arm *)

compound-op     ::= "UNION" [ "ALL" ] ;                                   (* V4 `UNION ALL` [parse.y:647
                                                                              multiselect_op UNION ALL]; V6 plain
                                                                              `UNION` [parse.y:646 multiselect_op
                                                                              UNION] added #377/#378 -- dedup via a
                                                                              shared ephemeral index across every
                                                                              arm, see codegen::select::
                                                                              compile_select_compound.
                                                                              `INTERSECT`/`EXCEPT`
                                                                              [parse.y:648 multiselect_op EXCEPT|
                                                                              INTERSECT] are parsed far enough to be
                                                                              reported `unsupported(..)` rather than
                                                                              a bare syntax error -- deferred to V7,
                                                                              see Future blocks below. *)

with-clause     ::= "WITH" [ "RECURSIVE" ] common-table-expr { "," common-table-expr } ;
                                                                          (* V6 [parse.y:601 with ::= WITH wqlist,
                                                                              :602 with ::= WITH RECURSIVE wqlist] --
                                                                              #375: only the non-recursive form is
                                                                              implemented; `WITH RECURSIVE` is parsed
                                                                              far enough to be reported
                                                                              `unsupported(..)` rather than a bare
                                                                              syntax error *)

common-table-expr ::= identifier [ "(" identifier { "," identifier } ")" ]
                       "AS" "(" select-stmt ")" ;
                                                                          (* V6 [parse.y:605 wqitem ::= nm eidlist_opt
                                                                              AS LP select RP, :592 eidlist_opt] --
                                                                              #375: the CTE name is parsed into
                                                                              `CommonTableExpr` but not yet resolvable
                                                                              in from-clause/table-ref -- materializing
                                                                              it as a queryable table is #376's scope *)

select-core     ::= "SELECT" [ "DISTINCT" | "ALL" ] result-column { "," result-column }
                     [ "FROM" from-clause ]
                     [ "WHERE" expr ]
                     [ group-by-clause ]
                     [ "HAVING" expr ] ;                               (* V2/V4/#287 -- HAVING is parse.y's own
                                                                            independent having_opt, not nested
                                                                            under groupby_opt: it's legal with no
                                                                            GROUP BY at all, filtering the
                                                                            implicit whole-table group (#287) *)

group-by-clause ::= "GROUP" "BY" expr { "," expr } ;
                     (* V4 [parse.y:950 groupby_opt]; HAVING [parse.y:955
                        having_opt] moved to select-core above -- #287 *)

result-column   ::= "*"                                   (* V2 [parse.y:717 selcollist] *)
                  | table-name "." "*"                    (* V2 [parse.y selcollist nm DOT STAR] *)
                  | expr [ [ "AS" ] identifier ] ;        (* V2 [parse.y:712 selcollist, :735 as] *)

table-ref       ::= table-name [ [ "AS" ] identifier ]    (* V2 [parse.y:762 seltablist, single-table shape only --
                                                              no schema-qualifier or INDEXED BY/NOT INDEXED] *)
                  | "(" select-stmt ")" [ "AS" ] identifier ;
                                                           (* V4 [parse.y:762 seltablist LP select RP as, #257 --
                                                              this codebase requires the alias SQLite itself
                                                              leaves optional, since column resolution needs a
                                                              qualifier] *)

(* #237 landed the INNER/plain JOIN, LEFT [OUTER] JOIN, and CROSS JOIN
 * subset of this chain; #250 extended it with NATURAL, RIGHT [OUTER]/
 * FULL [OUTER] JOIN, `USING (col, ...)`, and comma-style `FROM a, b` --
 * left-to-right -- `a JOIN b ON .. JOIN c ON ..` joins b against a,
 * then c against that result (`src/codegen/select.rs::compile_join_level`'s
 * nested-loop shape). A comma is sugar for an unconstrained CROSS JOIN
 * step in the same chain (`src/parser/grammar.rs::parse_from_clause`);
 * NATURAL joins take no ON/USING clause at all (the join columns are
 * same-named columns in both tables, resolved by codegen, not the
 * parser) and NATURAL CROSS JOIN is rejected (not legal SQLite
 * grammar -- CROSS is defined by the *absence* of a join condition,
 * which NATURAL's implied condition contradicts). A bare `JOIN`/
 * `INNER JOIN`/`LEFT/RIGHT/FULL [OUTER] JOIN` with no ON/USING (valid
 * SQL, a constraint-less cross join) is deliberately still
 * `unsupported(..)`. #250 is parser-only: codegen still only
 * implements INNER/LEFT/CROSS with an `ON` constraint --
 * `compile_select_joined` rejects NATURAL/RIGHT/FULL/USING joins with a
 * clean `Unsupported` error as an interim stopgap. NOT shared with
 * column-rs's own narrower `from-clause`/`join-kind` (section 2) --
 * see the file header.
 *)
from-clause     ::= table-ref { ( "," | join-op ) table-ref
                       [ "ON" expr | "USING" "(" column-name { "," column-name } ")" ] } ;
                                                                          (* V4 [parse.y:750 from, :758 stl_prefix,
                                                                              :762 seltablist, :894 on_using,
                                                                              :895 on_using USING] *)
join-op         ::= [ "NATURAL" ] [ "INNER" ] "JOIN"                     (* V4 [parse.y:866 joinop COMMA|JOIN,
                                                                              :868 joinop JOIN_KW JOIN] *)
                  | [ "NATURAL" ] "LEFT" [ "OUTER" ] "JOIN"               (* V4 [parse.y:870 joinop JOIN_KW nm JOIN] *)
                  | [ "NATURAL" ] "RIGHT" [ "OUTER" ] "JOIN"              (* V4 [parse.y:870 joinop JOIN_KW nm JOIN] --
                                                                              #250 *)
                  | [ "NATURAL" ] "FULL" [ "OUTER" ] "JOIN"               (* V4 [parse.y:872 joinop JOIN_KW nm nm JOIN] --
                                                                              #250 *)
                  | "CROSS" "JOIN" ;                                     (* V4 [parse.y:872 joinop] --
                                                                              CROSS JOIN takes no ON here, but
                                                                              #250 allows USING with it *)

ordering-term   ::= expr [ "COLLATE" collation-name ]
                     [ "ASC" | "DESC" ]
                     [ "NULLS" ( "FIRST" | "LAST" ) ] ;   (* V2 [parse.y:927 sortlist] *)

collation-name  ::= identifier ;                          (* V2: BINARY | NOCASE | RTRIM *)

(* ----- Expressions (V2 slice) -----
 * [parse.y:1176 expr, :1193 term]
 * Precedence, LOWEST to HIGHEST, mirrors parse.y's %left/%right
 * declarations [parse.y:295-309] exactly:
 *
 *   OR                                        [%left OR]
 *   AND                                       [%left AND]
 *   NOT (unary, prefix)                       [%right NOT]
 *   = == != <> IS ISNULL NOTNULL IN LIKE GLOB MATCH REGEXP BETWEEN
 *                                             [%left IS MATCH LIKE_KW BETWEEN IN ISNULL NOTNULL NE EQ]
 *   < <= > >=                                 [%left GT LE LT GE]
 *   ESCAPE                                    [%right ESCAPE]
 *   & | << >>                                 [%left BITAND BITOR LSHIFT RSHIFT]
 *   + -  (binary)                             [%left PLUS MINUS]
 *   * / %                                     [%left STAR SLASH REM]
 *   ||                                        [%left CONCAT]
 *   COLLATE (postfix)                         [%left COLLATE]
 *   ~ unary + unary -                         [%right BITNOT]
 *
 * `expr`/`or-expr`/`and-expr` below are textually IDENTICAL to column-
 * rs's own (section 2) -- deliberately NOT moved to SHARED; see the
 * file header. Everything from `not-expr` down is sqlite-rs-specific.
 *)

expr            ::= or-expr ;                                             (* V2 *)
or-expr         ::= and-expr { "OR" and-expr } ;                          (* V2 [parse.y:1349] *)
and-expr        ::= not-expr { "AND" not-expr } ;                         (* V2 [parse.y:1348] *)
not-expr        ::= { "NOT" } equality-expr ;                             (* V2 [parse.y:1448] *)

equality-expr   ::= relational-expr { equality-tail } ;                   (* V2 *)
equality-tail   ::= ( "=" | "==" | "!=" | "<>" ) relational-expr          (* V2 [parse.y:1350,1352]; `=`/`!=`/`<>`
                                                                              are 3 of SHARED `comparison-op`'s 7 --
                                                                              `==` is this engine's own extra synonym *)
                  | "IS" [ "NOT" ] relational-expr                        (* V2 [parse.y expr ::= expr IS expr] *)
                  | "ISNULL"                                              (* V2 [parse.y expr ::= expr ISNULL] *)
                  | "NOTNULL" | "NOT" "NULL"                              (* V2 [parse.y expr ::= expr NOTNULL] *)
                  | [ "NOT" ] "BETWEEN" relational-expr "AND" relational-expr
                                                                          (* V2 [parse.y expr ::= expr between_op] *)
                  | [ "NOT" ] "IN" "(" [ expr-list ] ")"                  (* V2 [parse.y expr ::= expr in_op LP exprlist RP] *)
                  | [ "NOT" ] "IN" "(" select-stmt ")"                   (* V4 [parse.y expr ::= expr in_op LP select RP] --
                                                                             #238: incl. correlated; IN table-name still stubbed below *)
                  | [ "NOT" ] like-op relational-expr [ "ESCAPE" relational-expr ] ;
                                                                          (* V2 [parse.y expr ::= expr likeop expr] *)
like-op         ::= "LIKE" | "GLOB" ;                                     (* V2; "REGEXP"|"MATCH" V7+ (need functions) *)

relational-expr ::= bitwise-expr { ( "<" | "<=" | ">" | ">=" ) bitwise-expr } ;  (* V2 [parse.y:1354]; the other 4
                                                                                    of SHARED `comparison-op`'s 7 *)
bitwise-expr    ::= additive-expr { ( "&" | "|" | "<<" | ">>" ) additive-expr } ; (* V2 [parse.y:1353] *)
additive-expr   ::= multiplicative-expr { ( "+" | "-" ) multiplicative-expr } ;   (* V2 [parse.y:1355] *)
multiplicative-expr ::= concat-expr { ( "*" | "/" | "%" ) concat-expr } ;         (* V2 [parse.y:1357] *)
concat-expr     ::= collate-expr { "||" collate-expr } ;                          (* V2 [parse.y:1359]; "->"/"->>" V11 *)
collate-expr    ::= unary-expr { "COLLATE" collation-name } ;             (* V2 [parse.y expr ::= expr COLLATE ids] *)
unary-expr      ::= { "+" | "-" | "~" } primary-expr ;                    (* V2 [parse.y:1452, BITNOT] *)

primary-expr    ::= literal-value                                         (* V2 [parse.y:1193-1204 term] *)
                  | parameter                                             (* V2 [parse.y expr ::= VARIABLE] *)
                  | column-ref                                            (* V2 [parse.y:1177 expr ::= idj] *)
                  | function-call                                         (* V2 [parse.y:1235] *)
                  | case-expr                                             (* V2 [parse.y expr ::= CASE case_operand case_exprlist case_else END] *)
                  | cast-expr                                             (* V2 [parse.y expr ::= CAST LP expr AS typetoken RP] *)
                  | "(" expr ")"                                          (* V2 [parse.y:1176] *)
                  | "(" select-stmt ")"                                   (* V4 [parse.y expr ::= LP select RP] --
                                                                             #238: scalar subquery, incl. correlated *)
                  | [ "NOT" ] "EXISTS" "(" select-stmt ")" ;               (* V4 [parse.y expr ::= EXISTS LP select RP] --
                                                                             #238: incl. correlated; NOT EXISTS is this crate's own negated
                                                                             variant, not a generic NOT wrapper *)
                  (* V9: RAISE *)

case-expr       ::= "CASE" [ expr ] ( "WHEN" expr "THEN" expr ) { "WHEN" expr "THEN" expr }
                     [ "ELSE" expr ] "END" ;                              (* V2 *)

cast-expr       ::= "CAST" "(" expr "AS" type-name ")" ;                  (* V2 *)

function-call   ::= identifier "(" [ [ "DISTINCT" ] expr-list | "*" ] ")" ;
                     (* V2 scalar only (count(*) V4 with aggregates)
                        [parse.y:1235 expr ::= idj LP distinct exprlist RP]
                        V9: FILTER clause, OVER window *)

literal-value   ::= NUMBER                                                (* V2: int, hex 0x, float incl. 1e5/.5/1. --
                                                                              richer than column-rs's own INTEGER/
                                                                              FLOAT (section 2); NOT shared *)
                  | STRING                                                (* V2: '...' with '' escaping -- NOT shared
                                                                              with column-rs's own STRING (section 2) *)
                  | BLOB                                                  (* V2: X'hex' *)
                  | "NULL" | "TRUE" | "FALSE"                             (* V2 *)
                  | "CURRENT_TIME" | "CURRENT_DATE" | "CURRENT_TIMESTAMP" ; (* V9: needs date/time fns *)

parameter       ::= "?" | "?" NUMBER | ":" identifier | "@" identifier | "$" identifier ;  (* V2 *)

column-ref      ::= identifier [ "." identifier [ "." identifier ] ] ;    (* V2 [parse.y:1178]; 3-part = db.tbl.col,
                                                                              ATTACH semantics V10. column-rs's own
                                                                              `column-ref` (section 2) allows only 2
                                                                              parts -- NOT shared. *)

expr-list       ::= expr { "," expr } ;                                   (* V2 [parse.y:1605 nexprlist] *)

(* ----- DML (V3 block) -----
 * Carried from the spike distillation; V3 tickets grow these
 * (upsert/RETURNING are V9).
 *)

insert-stmt     ::= "INSERT" [ "OR" conflict-action ] "INTO" table-name
                     [ "(" column-name { "," column-name } ")" ]
                     ( "VALUES" "(" expr-list ")" { "," "(" expr-list ")" }
                     | select-stmt
                     | "DEFAULT" "VALUES" ) ;                             (* V3 [parse.y:1082 cmd, :1114 insert_cmd, :1122 idlist_opt] *)

conflict-action ::= "REPLACE" | "IGNORE" | "ABORT" | "ROLLBACK" | "FAIL" ; (* V3 [parse.y:492 orconf, :493 resolvetype] *)

update-stmt     ::= "UPDATE" [ "OR" conflict-action ] table-name
                     "SET" set-assignment { "," set-assignment }
                     [ "WHERE" expr ] ;                                   (* V3 [parse.y:1014 cmd, :1065 setlist] *)

set-assignment  ::= column-name "=" expr
                  | "(" column-name { "," column-name } ")" "=" "(" expr-list ")" ;
                                                                           (* V3 [parse.y:1065 setlist, :1073 nm] *)

delete-stmt     ::= "DELETE" "FROM" table-name [ "WHERE" expr ] ;         (* V3 [parse.y:978 cmd, :1002 where_opt] *)

(* ----- DDL (V3 block) ----- *)

create-table-stmt ::= "CREATE" "TABLE" [ "IF" "NOT" "EXISTS" ] table-name
                       "(" column-def { "," column-def } [ "," table-constraint { "," table-constraint } ] ")"
                       [ "WITHOUT" "ROWID" | "STRICT" ] ;                 (* V3 [parse.y:207-247] *)

column-def      ::= column-name [ type-name ] { [ "CONSTRAINT" identifier ] column-constraint } ;
                                                                           (* V3 [parse.y:253 columnname, :389-424 ccons] *)

type-name       ::= identifier { identifier } [ "(" NUMBER [ "," NUMBER ] ")" ] ;  (* V3 [parse.y:356 typename] *)

column-constraint ::= "NOT" "NULL"                                        (* V3 [parse.y:389 ccons] *)
                    | "PRIMARY" "KEY" [ "ASC" | "DESC" ] [ "AUTOINCREMENT" ]  (* V3 [parse.y:392 ccons] *)
                    | "UNIQUE"                                            (* V3 *)
                    | "DEFAULT" ( literal-value | "(" expr ")" )          (* V3 *)
                    | "CHECK" "(" expr ")"                                (* V3 *)
                    | "COLLATE" collation-name ;                         (* V3; FK REFERENCES V8; GENERATED V10;
                                                                             ON CONFLICT resolution after NOT NULL/
                                                                             PRIMARY KEY/UNIQUE not yet supported *)

table-constraint ::= [ "CONSTRAINT" identifier ]
                     ( "PRIMARY" "KEY" "(" indexed-column { "," indexed-column } ")"
                     | "UNIQUE" "(" indexed-column { "," indexed-column } ")"
                     | "CHECK" "(" expr ")" ) ;                           (* V3; FOREIGN KEY V8 *)

indexed-column  ::= expr [ "ASC" | "DESC" ] ;                             (* V3 [parse.y sortlist];
                                                                             expr already covers optional COLLATE
                                                                             via collate-expr; no NULLS FIRST/LAST *)

create-index-stmt ::= "CREATE" [ "UNIQUE" ] "INDEX" [ "IF" "NOT" "EXISTS" ] identifier
                       "ON" table-name "(" indexed-column { "," indexed-column } ")"
                       [ "WHERE" expr ] ;                                 (* V3 [parse.y cmd ::= CREATE uniqueflag INDEX]; partial-index WHERE V4 planner *)

drop-stmt       ::= "DROP" ( "TABLE" | "INDEX" ) [ "IF" "EXISTS" ] identifier ;  (* V3 [parse.y cmd ::= DROP TABLE ifexists fullname] *)

create-view-stmt ::= "CREATE" "VIEW" [ "IF" "NOT" "EXISTS" ] view-name
                      [ "(" identifier { "," identifier } ")" ]
                      "AS" select-stmt ;
                                                                          (* V6 [parse.y:509-512 cmd] -- #379: the
                                                                              `AS select-stmt` body is the same
                                                                              select-stmt production as a top-level
                                                                              SELECT (no extra wrapping parens,
                                                                              matching real SQLite's grammar, unlike
                                                                              common-table-expr's `AS ( select-stmt )`
                                                                              form above); #380 is where the parsed
                                                                              view becomes queryable (sqlite_master
                                                                              storage + FROM-clause expansion) *)

drop-view-stmt  ::= "DROP" "VIEW" [ "IF" "EXISTS" ] view-name ;
                                                                          (* V6 [parse.y:513-515 cmd] -- #379 *)

view-name       ::= identifier ;                                         (* V6 *)

(* ----- PRAGMA journal_mode / integrity_check / quick_check
 *       / synchronous (V6/V7 carve-outs) -----
 * [parse.y:1715 cmd ::= PRAGMA nm dbnm EQ nmnum, :1717 cmd ::= PRAGMA nm
 * dbnm] -- `nmnum` (parse.y:1723) is where `DELETE`/`FULL` are folded in
 * as bare keyword values alongside a plain `nm` (identifier) value like
 * `WAL`/`OFF`/`NORMAL`, which is why the `journal_mode`/`synchronous`
 * value alternations below need their own literal cases for those two
 * rather than one `identifier` production.
 * `integrity_check`/`quick_check` (#540, #541) are the bare
 * `PRAGMA nm dbnm` form (no `EQ`) with no result-set arguments --
 * stock SQLite's `PRAGMA integrity_check(N)` (bounding the number of
 * errors reported) and any `dbnm` (schema-qualified) prefix are out of
 * this carve-out's scope. `synchronous` (#645) is the first pragma here
 * that's genuinely bidirectional: `PRAGMA synchronous` (no `EQ`, same
 * :1717 production as `integrity_check`) queries the connection's
 * current level instead of changing it -- `journal_mode`'s own bare
 * query form stays unimplemented (`Unsupported`). General PRAGMA
 * support -- every other pragma name, journal_mode's other
 * stock-SQLite values (MEMORY/OFF/TRUNCATE/PERSIST), and synchronous's
 * `EXTRA`/`ON`/boolean aliases and out-of-0-2-range integers (with
 * their own legacy masking quirks) -- stays deferred to V7 (see Future
 * blocks below); #388/#540/#541/#645 carve out only these cases.
 *)

pragma-stmt     ::= "PRAGMA" "journal_mode" "=" ( "WAL" | "DELETE" )
                   | "PRAGMA" ( "integrity_check" | "quick_check" )
                   | "PRAGMA" "synchronous" [ "=" ( "OFF" | "NORMAL" | "FULL"
                                                   | "0" | "1" | "2" ) ] ;
                     (* V6 [parse.y:1715 cmd] -- #388 *)
                     (* V7 [parse.y:1717 cmd] -- #540, #541 *)
                     (* V7 [parse.y:1715,1717 cmd] -- #645 *)

(* ----- ANALYZE (V7 carve-out) -----
 * [parse.y:1875 cmd ::= ANALYZE, :1876 cmd ::= ANALYZE nm dbnm] --
 * `dbnm` (an optional `.`-qualified schema-name prefix on the table
 * name) is a stock-SQLite production this MVP carve-out doesn't
 * implement: `table-name` below is a single bare identifier only, and
 * the parser reports `Unsupported` (not a hard parse error) if a `.`
 * follows it -- #461, spec 011.
 *)

analyze-stmt    ::= "ANALYZE" [ table-name ] ;
                     (* V7 [parse.y:1875 cmd] -- #461 *)

(* ----- Transaction control (V5 slice) -----
 * [parse.y:181 cmd ::= BEGIN transtype trans_opt, :186 transtype,
 *  :190 cmd ::= COMMIT|END trans_opt, :191 cmd ::= ROLLBACK trans_opt]
 * `ROLLBACK ... TO SAVEPOINT nm` (parse.y:201) and SAVEPOINT/RELEASE
 * themselves are out of scope here -- see Future blocks V5.
 *)

begin-stmt      ::= "BEGIN" [ "DEFERRED" | "IMMEDIATE" | "EXCLUSIVE" ] [ "TRANSACTION" ] ;
                     (* V5 [parse.y:181 cmd] *)

commit-stmt     ::= ( "COMMIT" | "END" ) [ "TRANSACTION" ] ;
                     (* V5 [parse.y:190 cmd] *)

rollback-stmt   ::= "ROLLBACK" [ "TRANSACTION" ] ;
                     (* V5 [parse.y:191 cmd] *)

(* ----- Lexical (sqlite-rs-specific) -----
 * [parse.y:365-368 %token_class id/ids/idj; keyword fallback to ID
 *  at parse.y:262-283 %fallback ID]
 *)

table-name      ::= identifier ;                                          (* V2 *)
column-name     ::= identifier ;                                          (* V2 *)
identifier      ::= ident-chars                                           (* bare -- see SHARED above *)
                   | quoted-identifier ;                                  (* V2 tokenizer (#60) *)
quoted-identifier ::= '"' { any character, `""` escapes a literal `"` } '"'
                     | "[" { any character except "]" } "]"
                     | "`" { any character, "``" escapes a literal "`" } "`" ;
                     (* NOT shared with column-rs's own `IDENT` (section
                        2), which is `ident-chars` alone. *)

(* ----- Future blocks (stubs -- the denominator) -----
 * V4:  subqueries in FROM (table-valued)
 *      (#237 landed the INNER/LEFT [OUTER]/CROSS `JOIN ... ON` subset,
 *      #250 landed NATURAL/RIGHT/FULL joins, USING (...), and
 *      comma-style FROM a, b -- see from-clause/join-op above),
 *      ANY/ALL/SOME quantified comparisons, multi-column IN
 *      (#238 landed scalar/IN/EXISTS subquery expressions, including
 *      correlated ones -- see equality-tail/primary-expr above;
 *      correlation is resolved at codegen time via a scope fallback, not
 *      a distinct grammar production; #240 landed the `UNION ALL`
 *      compound-select tail, #377/#378 landed plain `UNION` -- see
 *      select-stmt/compound-op above; `INTERSECT`/`EXCEPT` moved to V7,
 *      see compound-op above),
 *      (CREATE VIEW landed -- #379, see create-view-stmt/drop-view-stmt
 *      above under V6;
 *      GROUP BY / HAVING landed -- #239, see select-stmt/group-by-clause
 *      above; WITH/CTEs moved to V6 -- see with-clause/common-table-expr
 *      above)
 * V5:  SAVEPOINT/RELEASE, ROLLBACK ... TO SAVEPOINT
 *      (BEGIN/COMMIT/ROLLBACK landed -- #356, see begin-stmt/commit-stmt/
 *      rollback-stmt above)
 * V6:  WITH RECURSIVE (non-recursive WITH/CTEs landed -- #375, see
 *      with-clause/common-table-expr above; codegen materialization of a
 *      CTE as a queryable table is a separate ticket, #376; plain
 *      `UNION` landed -- #377/#378, see compound-op above; CREATE VIEW /
 *      DROP VIEW landed -- #379, see create-view-stmt/drop-view-stmt
 *      above; view storage + FROM-clause expansion is #380)
 * V7:  PRAGMA (general form -- every pragma name/value other than the
 *      narrow `journal_mode = WAL|DELETE` carve-out that landed under
 *      V6 -- see pragma-stmt above, #388); ANALYZE's bare/single-table-
 *      name forms landed -- see analyze-stmt above, #461 -- but
 *      `ANALYZE schema-name.table-name` and `ANALYZE index-name` stay a
 *      future-block stub (EXPLAIN [QUERY PLAN] pulled forward to V4 --
 *      #243), `INTERSECT`/`EXCEPT` compound-select ops (see compound-op
 *      above)
 * V8:  FOREIGN KEY refs + actions, CREATE TRIGGER (BEFORE/AFTER/INSTEAD OF, RAISE),
 *      ALTER TABLE (RENAME / ADD|DROP|RENAME COLUMN)
 * V9:  UPSERT (ON CONFLICT DO ...), RETURNING, window functions (OVER/PARTITION/frames),
 *      FILTER, CURRENT_* semantics
 * V10: WITHOUT ROWID/STRICT write semantics, GENERATED columns, ATTACH/DETACH,
 *      VACUUM [INTO], REINDEX
 * V11: CREATE VIRTUAL TABLE ... USING, "->" / "->>" JSON operators
 *)
